 OR(L("You are Deepak/Deepika. Write a letter to the Principal requesting a transfer certificate, as your father has been transferred to another city, in about 120 words.", "21, Gandhi Nagar<br>Jaipur<br>10 November 2026<br>The Principal<br>Mastermind Public School<br>Jaipur<br>Subject: Request for transfer certificate<br>Respected Madam/Sir,<br>I beg to state that my father has been transferred to Pune and our family will leave Jaipur on 30 November 2026. I therefore request you to issue my transfer certificate and other school records at an early date so that I can seek admission to a new school. I shall remain grateful to you for your guidance during my years in this school.<br>Yours obediently<br>Deepak/Deepika<br>Class 9 A" + FL),
    L("You are a resident of Sector 9. Write a formal email in about 120 words to the Electricity Board complaining about frequent power cuts in your locality.", "<b>To:</b> customercare@electricityboard.example<br><b>From:</b> resident.sector9@mail.example<br><b>Subject:</b> Frequent power cuts in Sector 9<br>Respected Sir/Madam,<br>I wish to bring to your notice that Sector 9 has been experiencing power cuts of three to four hours daily for the past fortnight. The cuts are unannounced and affect the studies of schoolchildren, who are preparing for examinations, and the work of shopkeepers and the elderly. I request you to look into the matter, repair the faulty transformer and publish a schedule if maintenance is needed. I shall be grateful for early relief.<br>Yours faithfully<br>A resident of Sector 9" + FL)),
 OR(A("Write a speech in about 120–150 words to be delivered in the assembly on 'The Value of Time'.", "<b>Time and Tide Wait for None</b><br>Good morning respected teachers and friends. The most valuable thing we own is neither money nor property, but time. Once spent, it can never be recovered. Every wasted hour is a lost opportunity, and every well-used hour is an investment.<br>Look at successful people. They rise early, plan their day and keep their promises. We too can begin with simple steps: make a timetable, finish today's work today and keep distractions like mobile phones away during study. Reserve some time for play and rest, for a tired mind cannot learn well.<br>Remember, the sands in the hourglass do not stop. Let us promise to use each moment wisely, so that when we look back, we may feel proud and not sorry. Thank you." + FA),
    A("Write an article in about 120–150 words for the school magazine on 'Social Media: A Boon or a Bane?'.", "<b>Social Media: Boon or Bane?</b><br>by Tanmay/Tanvi, Class 9<br>Social media has brought the world to our fingertips. We can talk to friends in another country, learn new skills from videos and share ideas with millions. During the pandemic it kept schools running and families connected.<br>Yet there is a darker side. Many students spend hours scrolling, losing sleep and concentration. Unkind comments and false news spread quickly and hurt innocent people. Some young users compare themselves with others and feel unhappy.<br>The answer lies in balance. Use social media for study and communication, but set a time limit. Think before posting and never share private details. Like fire, social media is a good servant but a bad master. If used wisely, it is certainly a boon." + FA)),
 OR(E("Write a narrative essay in 150–180 words on 'An Unforgettable Rainy Day'.", "It was Monday morning, and the sky was covered with dark clouds. I was just reaching the bus stop when thunder rolled, and large drops began to fall. Within minutes, the roads were flooded.<br>My bus did not come, so I and my friend Imran decided to walk. The water reached our knees, and our shoes were soaked. On the way we saw a small boy stuck on the footpath, crying for his mother. We took him by the hand and found his house, which was a few lanes away. His mother hugged us and offered hot tea and pakoras.<br>By the time we reached school, we were late and drenched, but the teacher, hearing our story, smiled and allowed us to sit down. Even now when I hear the sound of raindrops, I remember the little boy's relieved face. That rainy day taught me that small acts of kindness are the best prayers." + FE),
    E("Write a story in 150–180 words that ends with the line: '...and from that day onwards, he never told a lie again.'", "Mohan was a ten-year-old boy who liked to boast. One day he told his friends that he had seen a lion in the school garden. Everyone ran out in panic, and the teachers called the forest department. After a long search, the officers found nothing but a stray dog sleeping under a bush.<br>The Principal was angry and Mohan was scolded. He was embarrassed, but he soon forgot his lesson. A few days later, while playing, he fell into the school pond and cried, 'Help! Help!' His friends laughed and said, 'He is lying again.' It was only when the caretaker heard his screams and jumped in that Mohan was saved.<br>Shivering and ashamed, Mohan said, 'I am sorry. My lies almost cost me my life.' The caretaker patted his back kindly. Mohan kept his word, and from that day onwards, he never told a lie again." + FE)),
